          "language": "en",
          "section": "us_news",
          "raw_content": "I have a lot of mixed feelings about AI and LLM technology. Iâm fascinated by its effect on our profession, excited by the potential gains in productivity - and thus the products we could rapidly build. On the other hand, Iâm fearful of the damage AI might cause: agent swarms taking over our virtual and physical infrastructure, designing bio weapons. But, back on my first hand, LLMs might also design miracle cures, and come up with clever ways to raise our prosperity. Fundamentally I donât think we have a choice about riding on the AI technology train. Itâs a wild ride and I just hope weâll get through it OK. But as I mull on this more, I realize that among this mix of contrasting feelings, there is one emotion that dominates - one that comes from my direct interactions with LLMs. I donât like them. They talk to me in this grating LLM-voice, an uncanny valley of talking to a ",
